  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM EDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe upper flow flattens out over the next several days as the \nsoutheast ridge expands eastward and shortwave energy ejects out of \na deepening Pacific northwest trough.  Warm air advection ramps up \nthis weekend with increasing PWATs, likely bringing unsettled \nweather back into the Great Lakes.  Several shortwaves will ride \nalong a quasi-stationary boundary layed out across the area, \nresulting in several rounds of precipitation potential. Temperatures \nwill be on the way up, with above normal readings likely over the \nweekend into next week as the thermal ridge expands north and east. \n\nRain chances increase this weekend into early next week as \ndeveloping stronger warm air advection brings deeper moisture \ninto the Great Lakes. This will eventually bring PWATs of 200% \nof normal to the northern Great Lakes with rounds of \nshowers/storms moving across the area. Saturday remains \nquestionable as to how dry (or wet) it will be, as some models \ninitially take the heaviest precipitation south/west of our \narea, as the convection follows the thickness graident. The \ncurrent thinking will have shower/storm chances increasing \nnorth/west on Saturday afternoon, with higher POPS Saturday \nnight into Sunday. This unsettled pattern will continue into the\nfirst half of next week, as shortwave energy ejects out of the \ndeepening western trough, sliding along the northern periphery \nof the upper ridge and across the Great Lakes. This set up has \nsome classic signatures for heavy rain potential with a quasi-\nstationary boundary layed out across the Great Lakes, deep \nmoisture and favorable kinematics. \n\nWith regard to temperatures, an expanding thermal ridge will bring \nsummer heat and humidity back into the Great Lakes as we end August \nand head into September. This may result in highs into the 80s \non some days with overnight lows in the 60s. Air conditioning \nseason is not over yet. The main wildcard to temperatures \nthrough this time will be clouds and convection.",
